{
  "tool": "jev_find",
  "model": "jev-1.13.0",
  "provider": "typesafe",
  "query": "A bug makes the refund_allowed function violate the refund policy. docs/refunds.md says a refund may be requested within 30 days of delivery; the window starts on the delivery date, not the order date; the delivery date is day 0, a request on day 30 is on time and day 31 is late. Which candidate line is that refund bug?",
  "exists": 0.99,
  "exists_verdict": "answered",
  "top": [
    {
      "id": "refunds-return",
      "probability": 1,
      "text": "refunds.py line 12, the return in refund_allowed: `return (requested - ordered).days <= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS`. The function signature is refund_allowed(ordered: date, delivered: date, requested: date). It measures the window from the order date `ordered`, so slow shipping eats the customer's window: an order delivered 2026-03-05 may be refunded only through 2026-03-31, and a request on 2026-04-04 (day 30 after delivery) is refused. docs/refunds.md: a customer may request a refund within 30 days of delivery; the window starts on the delivery date, not the order date; delivery date is day 0; request on day 30 is on time, day 31 is late. This line is directly in refund_allowed and changes whether refunds are allowed."
    },
    {
      "id": "routing-rules",
      "probability": 0,
      "text": "routing.py line 5, the RULES table: tuples for billing (charge, refund, invoice, payment), then shipping (package, delivery, tracking, courier), then security (password, phishing). In route(), the first matching rule wins, so a ticket naming both a charge and a password goes to billing. docs/routing.md: security first; a ticket reporting unauthorized access goes to security whatever else it mentions, including a charge or refund. This line governs ticket routing, not refund eligibility or refund_allowed."
    },
    {
      "id": "installments-round",
      "probability": 0,
      "text": "installments.py line 10, the round call in split: `return [round(total_cents / parts)] * parts`. rounding total/parts to the nearest cent and repeating it does not make the parts add up to the total (e.g. 100 cents into 3 gives 33+33+33=99) and does not order larger parts first. docs/installments.md: installments add up to the order total exactly; no two differ by more than one cent; when they differ the larger installments come first. This line governs installment splitting, not refund eligibility or refund_allowed."
    }
  ],
  "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 1093,
    "output_tokens": 64
  },
  "request_id": "req_01a0e483cd0a7397b0d24d85d74f4270"
}

